From: Tristan Ward <14697631+WidgetKing@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 20 Sep 2026 21:21:20 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Click twice when the committing key is pressed again

A double click is the one thing a keyboard pointer could not say. Every
other gesture has somewhere to put the second half of itself -- a drag
has two passes, a hold has the keys you steer it with -- but a click
ends the run, and by the time anything could ask for a second one the
overlay is down and the keyboard is back where it came from.

So the run does not quite end. mode_click.double_click_ms keeps the
overlay alive for that long after a selection, watching for one key: the
very key that committed it. Press it again and the pointer clicks again.

That key is whatever finished the selection, which makes the gesture
read the same in both modes without either of them knowing about it. A
grid commits on space or Return, so a second space is a double click. A
hint commits on the last character of its label, so the hint `bd` is
double clicked by typing `b`, `d`, `d`, and `xyz` by `x`, `y`, `z`, `z`.
Neither mode is touched: both already funnel through the one place where
has_last_mode_returned() goes true, and that is where this listens.

The first click goes out at once rather than being held back until the
window closes, and that is the whole design. The first click of a double
click IS a single click -- a mouse does not know which one it is making
either -- so waiting to find out would put the window's full length in
front of every single click in the session to buy the occasional double
one. Emitted this way a double click costs nothing a single click does
not, and the two presses arrive a reaction time apart, which is what the
application is measuring anyway. The window therefore has to be shorter
than the double-click time of whatever is being clicked, because the
second click goes out when the key is pressed and not when the window
closes.

The bill for that is the keyboard. For as long as the window is open the
overlay still holds it, so a key typed straight after a click is eaten
instead of reaching what was clicked. Hence: any key that is not the
committing one closes the window immediately rather than being swallowed
for the rest of it. One lost keystroke in the worst case, and only when
something is typed inside the window.

What is on screen while it is open is not the overlay. Leaving the
labels up would hide the thing just clicked, which is also the thing
being decided about, so the surface is cleared and a ring is drawn round
the selection -- three rings of falling alpha, the way imthemousenow
draws the pointer during a hold, because a hold and this window are the
same kind of moment: the desktop looks normal while the keyboard does
not mean what it usually does.

Nothing arms when there is no click to double: move, both halves of a
drag and hold all select with CLICK_NONE, and --only-print has no
pointer to press with. double_click_ms defaults to 0, so a config that
does not ask for this behaves exactly as it did.

Two things had to move to make room. The two main loops became one: the
bare wl_display_dispatch used when the keyboard was the only source
cannot be given a deadline, and rather than keep two loops and teach
only one of them to wake up on time, the poll loop the key channel
already needed now runs in both cases -- with no channel its second fd
is simply -1. And clicks are emitted by the loop rather than by the key
handler that decided on them, because move_pointer round-trips the
display and a round trip inside a dispatch is a dispatch inside a
dispatch.

Not sent upstream yet.

